_1h_input_tokens": 5050}, "output_tokens": 6, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-29T08:47:00.620Z", "request_id": "req_011CgWEgPwgiRS4aSb9SMsjS", "thinking_duration_ms": 29208}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgWEgQCZoBvdi2ZsEjSwW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I traced the order patterns rather than doing a full day-by-day simulation, so these estimates are rough.\n\n**How the plans differ**\n- **Daily plans (0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6):** each one orders the forecast for the arrival day, multiplied by a fixed factor. The factors are about 0.9 for candidate 2, 1.0 for 0, 1.05 for 6, 1.1 for 5, 1.2 for 3 and 1.27 for 4. Average demand is about 1.046 × forecast, so plans 2 and 0 will often run short.\n- **Candidate 1:** orders every second day, so it pays the 26 order fee 13 fewer times (338 saved). Two-day batches of product 0 add about 300 in holding cost, and product 1 adds about 65. One batch covering two days of random demand also runs short less often than two separate orders. Its order sizes are about 1.1–1.14 × forecast.\n- **Candidate 7:** orders every third day, which saves about 440 in fees. Its larger batches add about 570 in holding cost for product 0 and about 135 for product 1, which outweighs the saving. On day 19 the warehouse would hold about 169 of its 172 places, so a high-demand stretch could push it over capacity and lose stock.\n\n**Costs of running short versus over-ordering**\n- **Product 0:** each missed sale costs 11 (5 lost margin p